WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.400
- [Narrator] During the
2008 presidential campaign,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.510
Michelle Obama was asked
on "The Tonight Show"

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.060
about the cost of her wardrobe.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.560
- [Jay] 60, 70,000 for that outfit?

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.670
- Actually, this is a J. Crew ensemble.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.370
- Really, wow.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.300
- [Narrator] J. Crew clothing
would become a staple

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.170
in the former first lady's wardrobe,

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.670
elevating the brand's
image from preppy basics

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.830
to professional, yet
affordable chic, but recently,

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.360
the apparel brand has
struggled with slumping sales

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.720
and a debt load that
ultimately led the retailer

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.450
to seek bankruptcy protection.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.070
In court filings, the
company cited losses incurred

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.900
because of coronavirus lockdowns,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.170
but analysts say the brand
also lost touch with consumers

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.640
with styles that appeared more suited

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.200
for the runway than for everyday life.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.513
Here's how J. Crew filed for bankruptcy.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.740
J. Crew, formerly known as
Popular Merchandise, Inc.,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.320
emerged in 1983 as a
catalog-only retailer.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.839
It offered a pioneering
line of casual fashion

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:58.020
built on affordable weathered chinos

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.160
and simple crew neck t-shirts.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.320
- Well, they were really
the preppy chic brand,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.340
sort of preppy with a twist,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.640
the kind of clothes that, you know,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.840
you could wear to the office,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.560
but also the perfect weekend clothes.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.770
- [Narrator] The brand opened its first

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.640
retail store in 1989,
and over the next decade,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.060
found its home in malls
across the country.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.370
Many of the brand's styles can
be attributed to Jenna Lyons

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.500
who was first hired as a designer in 1990.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.070
Her design influence grew
the brand by attracting both

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.400
luxury and middle-class customers.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.290
Former Gap CEO Mickey Drexler
joined the company in 2003

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.910
and promoted Lyons to creative
director a few years later.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.350
- Mickey Drexler was best
known for turning Gap

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.190
into a cultural icon in the 1990s.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.930
He came to J. Crew and
sort of had a second act,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.750
and Jenna Lyons was sort of his creative

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.680
right-hand woman, so to speak,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.630
to turn things like sequins
which you would think

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.260
were that you'd only were sort
of out on a Saturday night.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.050
All of a sudden, you could
wear them during the day,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.793
and she kind of really
glammed up the brand.

00:02:01.657 --> 00:02:03.650
- [Narrator] In 2006, Drexler founded

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.070
the successful companion brand Madewell.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.550
J. Crew also with public that same year

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.250
only to be taken private
again five years later

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.250
in a leveraged buyout that
saddled the company with debt.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.010
Not too long after, sales began to flag.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.620
Starting in 2014, J. Crew
experienced six straight years

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.150
of losses, and the company
started looking for ways

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.960
to cut back and attract new customers.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.500
- Well, they started
to get a bit too glam,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.070
and this is where some of the critics

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:33.730
started blaming Jenna, that
her style became too Brooklyn

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.000
for their kind of J. Crew's
mainstream customers,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.520
and then on top of that,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.590
they ended up with
quality and fit problems.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.640
The quality became inconsistent.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.950
Customers kind of just
lost faith in the brand.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.000
- [Narrator] In 2017 with the
company still floundering,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.370
Lyons departed, and Drexler
stepped down as CEO,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.990
and the brand narrowly
avoided bankruptcy as part of

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.030
a controversial debt swap that moved

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.630
its intellectual property
beyond the reach of creditors.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.650
- They were just caught
a little bit flat-footed

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.370
and didn't act fast enough to, you know,

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:11.160
develop e-commerce, to
develop all the things now

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.560
that we sort of expect from a retailer.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.100
- [Narrator] Heading into 2020,

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.500
J. Crew planned to raise
cash by spinning off

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.070
its Madewell chain through an IPO,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.760
but plans came to a halt
as coronavirus lockdowns

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.940
forced the retailer to
close some 500 stores.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.407
In court filings, the brand
said it expected to lose

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:31.930
$900 million in sales because of lockdowns

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.990
putting it in a position where
it couldn't pay its debts.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.890
On May 4th, 2020 J. Crew
filed for bankruptcy.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.460
- You know, J. Crew
has a smaller footprint

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.590
than a lot of other retailers,
and they didn't overexpand,

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.570
so that's a positive for them.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.300
They are in a position where
they're not gonna probably

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.580
have to close hundreds of stores,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.690
and they also have a
new CEO, and you know,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.040
her mission was sort
of to restore the brand

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.610
to its former glory,
getting back to the quality

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.180
and the fit that the customers loved

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.590
and kind of the classic styles.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.570
- [Narrator] As the retailer
looks to navigate its way

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.350
out of bankruptcy, it will
also try to recapture customers

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.290
who once looked for
clothes that could be worn

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:14.256
on any occasion, even inauguration day.

00:04:14.256 --> 00:04:18.109
(people cheering)
(anxious music)

